/*
    To convert the Semi-Formalized Structure into a Formalized Structure, we will examine the natural language clauses in the Semi-Formalized Structure one by one, and formalize each into a formal clause that is well-formed and well-defined according to the guidelines.
*/

{
    "premises": [
        /*
        The clause "W and Y are distinct points on line WY" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `distinctPointsOnLine`.

        We double check the description of the `distinctPointsOnLine` relation: "points a and b are distinct and on line L". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        It takes (a b : Point) (L : Line) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as: `distinctPointsOnLine W Y WY`.

        The clauses "S and Z are distinct points on line SZ", "T and V are distinct points on line TV" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the first 3 clauses as follows:
        */
        "distinctPointsOnLine W Y WY",
        "distinctPointsOnLine S Z SZ",
        "distinctPointsOnLine T V TV",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct lines WY and SZ intersect at point X" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint`.

        We double check the description of the `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint` relation: "distinct lines L1 and L2 intersect at point i". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause since it guarantees that the lines are distinct.

        It takes (L1 L2 : Line) (i: Point) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as: `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint WY SZ X`.

        The clause "The distinct lines TV and SZ intersect at point U" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the next 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "twoLinesIntersectAtPoint WY SZ X",
        "twoLinesIntersectAtPoint TV SZ U",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct points S, U, X, Z are collinear and ordered in this sequence from S to Z" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        We notice that this clause is equivalent to 2 betweenness relations: "U is between S and X, and they are mutually distinct points" and "X is between U and Z, and they are mutually distinct points", which have a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `between`.

        We double check the description of the `between` relation: "mutually distinct points a, b and c are collinear and ordered. Point b is between point a and c". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause since it guarantees that the points are mutually distinct.

        It takes (a b c : Point) as arguments in order, with Point b being the one that is between Point a and Point c.

        Therefore, we can formalize this clause as follows:
        */
        "between S U X ∧ between U X Z",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct points T and W are on the same side of line SZ, both not on line SZ" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `sameSide`.

        We double check the description of the `sameSide` relation: "point a and b are on the same side of line L. They both are not on line L, but can be the same point". It is not exactly equivalent to our target clause since it does not guarantee that the points are mutually distinct, but we can still use it to formalize the clause by adding an additional clause `T ≠ W` to guarantee distinctness.

        The `sameSide` relation is defined in the namespace `Point` with the syntax `a.sameSide b L`, so we can formalize the clause as: `T.sameSide W SZ ∧ T ≠ W`.

        The clause "The distinct points V and Y are on the same side of line SZ, both not on line SZ" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the next 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "T.sameSide W SZ ∧ T ≠ W",
        "V.sameSide Y SZ ∧ V ≠ Y",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct points T and V are on opposing sides of line SZ, both not on line SZ" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `opposingSides`.

        We double check the description of the `opposingSides` relation: "distinct point a and b are on opposite sides of line L. They both are not on line L". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause since it guarantees that the points are mutually distinct.

        The `opposingSides` relation is defined in the namespace `Point` with the syntax `a.opposingSides b L`, so we can formalize the clause as: `T.opposingSides V SZ`.

        The clause "The distinct points W and Y are on opposing sides of line SZ, both not on line SZ" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the next 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "T.opposingSides V SZ",
        "W.opposingSides Y SZ",

        /*
        The clause "The angles ∠ U X W and ∠ T U X are supplementary" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        The definition of supplementary angles is that the sum of their measures is 180°. In our case, "The angles ∠ U X W and ∠ T U X are supplementary" is equivalent to "∠ U X W + ∠ T U X = 180°".

        We notice that, following the instructions, the formal notation of an angle fromed by vertex b and sides a and c is `∠ a:b:c`, so we can directly formalize the clause using the numerical operator `+`, the constant `∟` representing a right angle (90°), and the equality relation `=` as follows:
        */
        "∠ U:X:W + ∠ T:U:X = ∟ + ∟"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
        /*
        The clause "The lines WY and TV are parallel, denoted as W Y ∥ T V" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        We notice that this clause is equivalent to "WY and TV do not intersect", which has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `intersectsLine`.

        We double check the description of the `intersectsLine` relation: "two lines L and M intersect at some point. They can be the same line". It is okay that `intersectsLine` allows the lines to be the same since we are going to negate it, which guarantees that the lines are mutually distinct.

        The `intersectsLine` relation is defined in the namespace `Line` with the syntax `L.intersectsLine M`, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "¬ WY.intersectsLine TV"
    ]
}
